## {#today .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.todaymeta}
A full-day workshop · four parts
:::

:::: {.todaybox}
::: {.nonincremental}
1. Credibility and tools – science, its crisis, and the toolchain
2. The claim and its estimand – the paper in full, estimands, and causal graphs
3. Reproduction and exploration – reproducing a published result, and working through the specification menu
4. Multiverse and preregistration – reading a specification curve, and declaring one analysis in advance
:::
::::

::: {.todaytake}
You leave with: a reproduced result · an estimand and a DAG of your own · a preregistered analysis · your dot on a live class multiverse
:::

::: {.todaymeta}
Work along in your IDE, solo or in pairs, with the browser lab page as the fallback
:::

::: notes
Welcome – good to have you here. A full day, in four parts of ninety minutes. The first part sets up everything else: what we are doing when we do social science, why people say it is in crisis, what open research proposes to do about that, and – because none of it happens without software – the tools. The second part goes inside one published paper that I re-analysed myself, and asks why five analysts of the same claim came back with five different answers. The third part is the coding-heavy one, in which you reproduce my submitted result, then work through the menu of choices you could have made instead. And in the fourth we read what the class built, and ask how you settle on the one analysis you would actually publish.
:::

## Two kinds of aim {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**What I want you to understand**

- What reproducibility, robustness and replicability each mean, and why they come apart
- Why analysts disagree – and how much of that is disagreement about the *question*
- What a causal graph is good for when you cannot make a causal claim
- What a multiverse shows, and what it cannot show
- What preregistration settles, and what it leaves open
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**What I want you to be able to do**

- Get an analysis running from someone else's materials
- Write an estimand in one sentence, and a DAG that matches it
- Reproduce a published result to the third decimal
- Vary a specification one choice at a time and read what changed
- Declare an analysis before you run it, and report where it ranks among the alternatives
:::
:::
::::

::: notes
Two columns, because the course has two kinds of aim. On the left is what I want you to understand – conceptual work, and most of it is not about code at all. On the right is what I want you to be able to do, and every one of those is something you will have done at least once with your own hands before we finish. If you only take the left-hand column home you will have heard some interesting arguments and changed nothing about your practice. If you only take the right-hand column you will have learned some R. What I want is the two together, because the concepts tell you which of the things you can now do is worth doing.
:::

