::: notes
And the last group: where things go so they are still there in ten years. OSF is free, holds projects and files and registrations, and the whole public record of our case is kept there – you will be reading it later. Zenodo is run by CERN, gives you a DOI, and will take a GitHub release directly, which makes archiving a one-click habit. Dataverse is the institutional data-archive world with proper metadata and curation. And then the domain archives – GESIS, the UK Data Service, ICPSR – which is where licensed survey microdata is held, including the Eurobarometer data behind our paper. A repository is not an archive, although people often treat it as one. A GitHub repository can have its history rewritten, be renamed or be deleted tomorrow, and the link in your paper dies. An archived deposit has a DOI, a fixed version and an institution committed to serving it. Serious work usually needs both.
:::

## Licences, briefly {.smaller .deepdive run="tools"}

::: {.small}
| What | Common choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Code** | MIT · GPL | MIT: use it however you like. GPL: and share your changes back |
| **Data** | CC0 · CC BY | CC0 puts it in the public domain; CC BY asks for attribution |
| **Text, figures, slides** | CC BY · CC BY-NC | Attribution, optionally no commercial reuse |
| **Someone else's data** | *their* terms | Eurobarometer is GESIS-licensed – I may not redistribute it, and neither may you |
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Publishing with no licence is not a neutral default. It means **all rights reserved** – legally, nobody may reuse the work at all.
:::

::: notes
Licensing gets skipped and then bites. Four rows. For code, MIT says use it however you like, GPL says use it and share your changes back. For data, CC-zero puts it in the public domain and CC-BY asks for attribution. For text and figures and slides, CC-BY or CC-BY with a non-commercial clause. And the fourth row is the one that constrains this course: when the data are not yours, their terms govern. The Eurobarometer microdata behind our paper is GESIS-licensed – I am not allowed to redistribute it, which is why you will be working on an aggregate I built and am allowed to share, and it is also the reason why the first SCORE attempt to reproduce this paper failed, which we come back to when we reach the paper itself. And the line at the bottom: leaving a repository without a licence does not make it free to use. Legally it means all rights reserved, so nobody may reuse it at all.
:::

