## Causal identification in this design {#we-cannot-identify-a-causal-effect-here .smaller}

::: {.small}
Observational panel · 27 countries · no assignment mechanism · feedback between the variables · a decade of shared shocks.

Nothing in this design licenses "unemployment **causes** a shift in EU framing".
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
So why draw a causal graph at all?
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Because the graph still does three jobs that do not need identification:

- It says **which association** you are after – for instance the total effect, rather than whatever the software returns
- It tells you **which variables belong in the model**, and, just as usefully, which do not
- It makes your assumptions **legible and arguable** – a reader can disagree with an arrow, and that is a productive disagreement
:::

::: notes
The template you have just filled in speaks of an expected change in framing for a one-percentage-point difference in unemployment, which sounds causal, so it is worth being plain about what this design can deliver. This is an observational panel of twenty-seven countries. Nobody assigned unemployment to anyone. The variables plausibly feed back into each other over time, and the whole decade is shot through with shocks that hit everybody at once. Nothing in that design licenses the sentence "unemployment causes a shift in EU framing". [advance] So why draw a causal graph at all? [advance] Because the graph does three jobs that do not need identification. It says which association you are actually after – the total effect, say, rather than whatever the software happens to return. It tells you which variables belong in your model, and just as usefully which do not. And it puts your assumptions on paper where a reader can disagree with a specific arrow, which is a more productive argument to have than one about a coefficient.
:::

## Publishing the estimand and the graph {#say-it-in-public-or-it-does-not-count .smaller}

::: {.small}
The graph and the estimand are only worth anything if they are **written down where someone else can read them**:

- in the paper itself
- **before** the model runs, not reconstructed afterwards
- with the **limitations named by you** before a reviewer finds them
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
In Task 5 you write all of that down – the estimand, the graph-based justification, the one specification, and the rule for what would count as support – and you do it **before** you see a result.
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The technical name for that is preregistration, and it fixes the question before anyone knows the result.
:::

::: notes
One more step follows from this, and it is where the tasks are heading. A graph you drew and did not publish is worth almost nothing. It has to be written down in the paper, where someone else can read it. It has to be written before the model runs, not reconstructed afterwards once you know which way the coefficient went, because a graph drawn after the fact will very reliably justify the result you already have. And you have to name the limitations yourself, before a reviewer finds them. [advance] In Task 5 you will write all of that down: your estimand, your graph-based justification, the one specification you commit to, and your rule for what would count as support – and you will do it before you see a result. [advance] The technical name for that is preregistration.
