## Two points from the literature {#two-sentences-worth-keeping}

::: {.claimcard}
"the common single-path analyses in social and behavioural research should not be simply assumed to be robust to alternative analyses" [@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness]
:::

::: {.claimcard}
A blueprint: precise question → causal reasoning (DAG) → multiverse → sensitivity analysis [@AuspurgBruderl2021HasCredibilitySocial]
:::

::: {.small}
- Same-conclusion robustness and same-*number* robustness are different bars
- Justifiability is the criterion – choose with reasons, show the spread
:::

::: notes
The first point comes from the Multi100 paper: single-path analyses should not simply be assumed robust. And notice what preregistration did and did not do for you today. It protected your choice from being opportunistic, but it gave your declared dot no special status inside the curve. The declaration made your one path defensible, and the grid showed that it was still one path among 840, so a report needs the declared path and the space around it together. From Auspurg and Brüderl comes the constructive recipe we followed – precise question, explicit assumptions, multiverse, sensitivity. And keep the two robustness standards apart in your own writing: same *conclusion*, which was seventy-four per cent in Multi100, and same *number*, which was thirty-four. When you claim robustness, say which one you mean.
:::

